Z||000000||fotofrank||11-11-2000||12:13 PM|||| How do I get the key out of the ignition ? I belive a rod goes up the stearing collum but I'm not sure. Car starts fine.<P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://fotofrank.homestead.com/foto1~ns4.html||||reg||9
Z||000001||deceler8||11-11-2000||12:26 PM||||This happened to me last summer. About all you can do is spray the ignition lock with WD40 and then try jiggling the key while turning the wheel/moving the gearshift.<P>It's frustrating, but you'll probably get it out. And when you do, spray the ignition switch with WD40 and get a new key.<P>Ever since this has happened, I've not had a problem with a new key.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||11-11-2000||12:27 PM||||On my manual Fiero there is a lever that you depress to allow you to remove the key from the ignition, and I've had cars that when the wheels are cranked to one side, the steering wheel locks up and you can't remove the key, or if the car is an automatic and the transmission is not in park, the key won't come out. Just some places to look.||||reg||1
Z||000003||martin1875||11-11-2000||12:34 PM||||have you thought about changing the lock with a new one. it pretty easy, and cheap auto zone sells the lock and key for about $10.00 in crome or about $14.00 in black.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||11-11-2000||01:19 PM||||Is this an automatic? there is a park interlock to the shifter if it is. push the shifter all the way forward. If the shifter is binding or out of adjustment it can hange the interlock. More information on the interlock is in my cave here.... <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Fiero Cave</A> it's in the steering column article which was updated recently.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fldevel||11-11-2000||10:09 PM||||i have the oppisite problem ..i can take the key out without having the key in the "unlock position" the only problem wioth this is if i dont turn the key all the way back i can sart the car without the key ||||reg||1
Z||000006||MrPBody||11-11-2000||11:19 PM||||Yes, a rod connects the lock assembly and the ignition switch (a narrow ceramic box mounted on top of the steering column.)  If the ignition switch is not adjusted properly, that can cause difficulty removing the key.<BR>||||reg||1
